  "Notes": [
    {
      "Text": "Security update for openssh",
      "Title": "Topic",
      "Type": "Summary"
    },
    {
      "Text": "This update for openssh fixes the following issues:\n\n- CVE-2026-59995: sftp: a malicious server could change the the location of downloaded files (bsc#1271044).\n- CVE-2026-59996: scp: a malicious server could write files to the parent directory of the intended target directory\n  (bsc#1271046).\n- CVE-2026-59997: sshd: internal-sftp command lines are silently truncated after the 9th argument (bsc#1271048).\n- CVE-2026-59998: sshd: Undocumented GSSAPIStrictAcceptorCheck behavior impacts security in Windows Active Directory\n  (bsc#1271049).\n- CVE-2026-59999: sshd: DisableForwarding=yes didn't override PermitTunnel=yes (bsc#1271052).\n- CVE-2026-60000: sshd: pre-authentication denial of service when GSSAPIAuthentication is enabled (bsc#1271053).\n- CVE-2026-60001: sshd: honor the minimum authentication delay (bsc#1271054).\n- CVE-2026-60002: ssh: client-side use-after-free when a server changes its host key during a key reexchange\n  (bsc#1271055).\n- CVE-2026-73281: refused binding requests on locked SSH agents can allow destination restriction bypasses\n  (bsc#1275075).\n- CVE-2026-73282: unsynchronized remote forwarding updates in SSH client multiplexing can cause use-after-free\n  (bsc#1275074).\n- CVE-2026-73283: unenforced restriction keywords on tunnel forwarding can allow administrative security policy bypasses\n  (bsc#1275079).\n",
      "Title": "Details",
      "Type": "General"
    },
    {
